A||||24||Adrift||Safest break upgrade?||||1||
Z||000000||Adrift||06-30-2001||09:11 PM||||So it has been more or less agreed upon that the Fiero breaks aren't the best breaks around... Sine people also say that a GrandAm conversion in fact is less safe then the standard fiero due to misweighted breaking in the front and rear (Sorry for the wrong terminology - more info @ <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007762-2.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/007762-2.html</A>  ) . So here is my question: what's the safest economical way to get better STREET breaking out of a Fiero?<P>Also, I was wondering about whether or not I should buy cross-drilled rotors. From what I've been told, they don't reduce the heat generated by the break, and if they are cross drilled wrong they can crack on you causing huge huge problems. Should I avoid cross drilled rotors? (The cross drilled rotors I am refering too are from such comapnies as the Fiero Store, where they take stock rotors and drill them themselves)||||reg||1
Z||000001||PBJ||06-30-2001||09:21 PM||||Cross drilled rotors are not for heat exchange but for weight reduction.<P>I have GA brakes on one of our cars and there is nothing like it.  Lots of people knock em but I doubt they have driven one with them.  People love to talk about GA brakes on a fiero and how they don't work but come on up and drive ours and they will soon disregard the math and say-WOW that is great brakes.  YES you do loose the park brake but how many people out there have a working park brake or would even pay to have their's repaired. <P>GA brakes work great.  Better results than you could even hope for.<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Adrift||06-30-2001||09:31 PM||||Isn't a function parking break required by law in Ontario? And the math (correct me if I'm wrong) refers to how the breaks perform in an extreme situation, which is what I'm worried about. Its logical that the GA break conversion will make your car stop quicker (The GA is bigger and heavier then the Fiero, so it should make natural sense that if you take breaks designed for a heavy car and put them on a light car the light car will stop faster. Correct me if I'm wrong on this too I'm just trying to use logic).<P>For example - wet highway, speeds of lets say 85kmph (We're driving slower because of bad conditions). A car in front, for one reason or another, stops or slows down very suddenly. Now in this situation it would be worse to spin out of control, which I believe the math says will happen, rather then rear end the person. <P>Now I'm just trying to use logic here so I may be wrong, but to me it seems that I don't need amazing breaks for everyday driving, I need amazing breaks for the emergencys.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Her86GT||06-30-2001||09:34 PM||||WELL!  I'm almost offended with all those out there that bash these Grand Am brake upgrades.  My husband is a GM tech. and he put the conversion all the way around on my car.  It brakes better than our '99 GMC Jimmy.  ON A DIME I tell ya!  I have never locked the front up before the back or had any type of balance problem.  GM put Chevette brakes on the front of Fieros and Camero brakes on the rear - right from the factory!  Remember, the Fiero didn't have it's OWN distinct braking system until 1988.  <P>If you haven't driven one with the upgrade, MAYBE YOU SHOULD!!! before you are convinced otherwise by people out there who are afraid to do it to there cars or have overly analyzed the upgrade and have themselves freaked out by the numbers.  IT WORKS and if you want to test drive mine, SURE   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mycar2sm.jpg"> ||||reg||4
Z||000004||Adrift||07-01-2001||12:24 AM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||07-01-2001||12:35 AM||||Most of the people that seem to put down the Grand Am conversion do so out of liabillity reasons...<BR>I could tell you that I have the rears converted on one of my Fieros, but I will not say YOU should do so, it is your choice...<BR>I can't wait to do the fronts now!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000006||8Ball||07-01-2001||12:59 AM||||Is there a way to do the Grand Am conversion and still retain a parking brake? Here in TN you REALLY need a p-brake seeing as how this town anyway is in the mountains, and besides I would NEVER park a stick with out the p-brake.<P>8Ball||||reg||1
Z||000007||JayUSA77||07-01-2001||01:04 AM||||8ball put it in gear and it shouldnt roll||||reg||3
Z||000008||theogre||07-01-2001||01:32 AM||||adrift, For more info about drill/slot visit <A HREF="http://www.baer.com" TARGET=_blank>www.baer.com</A>  or <A HREF="http://www.wilwood.com" TARGET=_blank>www.wilwood.com</A>  There are links to more information in my cave article.<P>They are waste management items and/or weight reduction items. They generally will not improve stopping distance.<P>For certain others....<P>As a "GM tech" he should know better than to intall an illegal upgrade on a car. You use what ever logic makes you feel good to forget that small fact.<P>There is not one source of information I can find anyplace, other than anecdotal reports from unqualified people, that supports any claim that grand am parts are even close to correct for both axles. So far only One person has provided any hard data that they help in Race applications.<P>The reason some of us "do the math" is because of people who should know better but obviously don't. We also do things like talk to real experts in the industry. People like Baer and Wilwood know far more than your so called profesional GM tech and they think you are all nuts putting that 4 wheel grand am setup on the car.<P>There is no way a car with 4 wheel grand am "ugrade" can be made safe or legal.<P>But oh,,, we can just throw in a new proportioning valve.... No you can't. Prop valves can and do fail. worse they often fail slowly over time. You may not even know they are gone until the car ends up spinning out. Once a prop valve is failed only the "natural" balance of the system is left to keep you from backing into a wall or worse.<P>Hand brakes are federally mandated. It's not up to the states. Do you think GM would put them in unless they are required to. You're talking about one of the cheapest bunch of A--holes on the planet... there are plenty of places where the hand brake is part of state inspection. Try getting thru an honest NY inspection without one. <P>At best the 4 wheel grand am conversion is a track only setup. Even in that arena Baer and Wilwood don't like the system but can understand reasons why it is done.<P>People who make remarks about the people who do the math and over analyze, obviusly didn't read the whole article.<P>Work on developing a safe, reliable, brake package is anything but done.<P>The current "package" in the works puts new calipers and rotors on the front. Most likely a Grand Am caliper but a rotor that allows proper machining of the hub.<P>It will likely replumb the lines to feed the primary MC outlet to the front. This isn't totally set yet. There are Two versions of OE rear piston. I haven't yet determined how, if at all, they difer in volume required to close them.<P>It will rework the OE prop valve to work right with the new brake balance. The OE valve has a safety system built in that after market ones do not. (Read Lawrence Mazza's brake article at Web electric. Do a search. the link is hear in many posts.)<P>it will leave the OE rear brake in place. Most people think solid rotors on the rear are automatically bad... most people need to learn more about brakes. The OE rear brakes are not performing at anywhere near their limit. (except maybe in totaly race  situations.) In fact the OE rear rotor is already heavier than the front one. It's as heavy as most of the rear Solid rotors on the majority of rear disk cars built today.<P>With a few exceptions, most cars have solid rear rotors. Keep in mind, rear brakes only provide about 30% of the braking power on a car. They simply do not generate the heat that front brakes do.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||artherd||07-01-2001||03:53 AM||||In my opinion, there are only two choices for brakes on a fiero.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.heldmotorsports.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.heldmotorsports.com</A> <P>Their 12 or 13" setup :)<P>Because your life (and maybe mine!) depends on it.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000010||Eric||07-01-2001||08:09 AM||||Ogre, have you experimented with an adjustable proportioning valve yet?  We have one on our cobra, and it seems to work very well on that bastard of a braking system.  The car has mustang brakes in front and t-birds in the back.  Stopping distances are around 105ft 60-0.  You're going to have a hard time convincing people the grand am swap is anything other than a good thing if all people have to do is mash the pedal to see that they're car's stopping faster.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Adrift||07-01-2001||10:40 AM||||Ogre, So is there anyway to safely increase bracking power for under $1000 US ? (except to "refurbish" your current break system by replacing calipers, pads, lines, etc...)||||reg||1
Z||000012||btoth||07-01-2001||11:11 AM||||How about Achie's kit that he occasionally posts pics of?||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||07-01-2001||12:12 PM||||Adrift, What is it you are doing with the car that makes you feel it needs a brake rework?<P>If you have exisitng problems, even small ones, the OE brakes will feel even worse than their weak design may make them to begin with. <P>If the pads/rotors/calipers are ok, the main thing to do is check, if need be replace, rubber brake lines. These can swell internally if oil hase soaked thru the outer skin. This will mess up brake pressure at the wheels. (If you replace any lines,,, flush new fluid thru the hard lines to remove trash. take off and drain the calipers. You can get almost every drop of old fluid out that way.)<P>Bleed the system and flush in plenty of new fluid. Plan on use as much as 1/2 gallon for the whole car. Use good fluid like Valvoline Synthetic or a similar very high temp DOT3 compatible fluid. <P>To clear the front calipers more comletely of old fluid, compress the pistons like you would for new pads. Do this with the bleeder open so the old fluid exits the system instead of backing up to the MC. You won't get every drop from the calipers but it will be enough to clean out most of the trash and get the entire front end allot of new fluid.<P>Use an old "trukey baster" to suck most of the old fluid from the MC before you start bleeding. make sure the MC stays full.<P>The rear calipers can't easily be drained witout removing them from the car/lines. If you just bleed them you'll at least clear the lines of crud and get some new fluid into the calipers.<P>Yes, the GA fronts do provide more power. And that is a factor. Allot of the inprovment people are claiming is also becuase durring the upgrade they replaced faulty parts and bled the system.<P>Baer reports that they have found no performance diferance between OE rubber lines (In good condition!) and braided stanless lines. Use whichever you feel like.<P>Once you've eliminated line, fluid, and any MC issues, then worry about changing parts at the wheels. The biggest problem with brakes is people not doing basic maintenance. Brake fluid has always beed a service item but until recently it's not listed in the car makers scheduled service charts. Brake Fluid should be replaced every couple years. (Read the Motor Age article. Link to it in my brake pages. or goto Motorage.com and search for "DOT 3")<P>Eric, I've used adjustable prop's before. They are made to fine tune a system that is otherwise correct for the car. They allow you to taylor the brakes for diferant tire combinations(and wear), track surfaces, and that sort of thing. They can even be installed in the cockpit to allow adjustment durring a race.<P>In kit cars, which all to often have improperly configured brakes because of mixed parts from multiple cars, they are used to try to correct a system that isn't right. As described above... This creates a false sense that the car is now totally safe. It's more stable in the short term but long term reliability will always be questionable.<P>If it's an original cobra, then you're mainly fine tuning as I mentioned above. Or at least you should be. If the OE brakes are batty, see if you can find out just why. then see if there's a more permanent fix.<P>In the case of many kits, both Wilwood and Baer can build systems that truely fix the problems. That's because kits often have certain things in common. like a tendancy to use allot of MustangII front ends with Ford or Dayna and some Chevy rear axles.<P>Fiero's problem is simply lack of volume to justify production runs. I'm sure Baer and Wilwood could make the parts needed but they won't likely be cheap. Baer even makes their own hubs to replace the rotor/hub like Fiero uses.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||West Coast Fiero||07-01-2001||02:22 PM||||If its any help to you guys, we also offer brake upgrades as well<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||Adrift||07-01-2001||02:34 PM||||Eric - I'm a little mixed up. On your breaks page ( <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/Brakes/Brakes.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/Brakes/Brakes.html</A>  ) you say that your kit uses "proven GM heavy duty brake components", meaning stock GM parts. Now correct me if I'm wrong, but what this whole thread is about is the danger in taking stock GM parts and adapting them to the fiero, a car they were not designed for? Basically what I'm asking is whats the difference inbetween your break conversion and the one we have been discussing in this thread?<p>[This message has been edited by Adrift (edited 07-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||theogre||07-01-2001||07:42 PM||||Adrift,<P>I'm not going to get into specific of Criss' system. I simply don't have anything but his published data. A major dif right off is that the system keeps the hand brake alive. That's a good thing.<P>If you look at his published specs, you'll see right off that there is still a "natural" balance to the system. In other words the front caliper piston is parger than the rear. This is diferant from the 4 wheel grand am setup which has no natural balance, all 4 pistons are the same size.<P>Cris, <P>If the picture of the MC prop valve is typical of your system installs, could you explain how you proportion that setup? putting the a second prop valve into the front brakes seems a bit odd. seems like it would cause the brake light to come on the way it's shown in your picture. Did you alter the rear prop valve in the balance block?||||reg||1
Z||000017||Jaxx||07-02-2001||09:20 PM||||theogre,<P>I did read through the information in your articles and followed up on some of the other links from your site. I've got an '86 GT with the Blazer MC and GA calipers on the rear, and have the calipers to do the fronts (bought 'em with the car). <P>You've got a lot of compelling data in the article, but the info raises an interesting question. Just what is the "natural" balance of the braking system supposed to be? You suggest that the main stopping power comes from the fronts, leaving a reserve in the rear system in case of emergency/failure. I'm still debating on putting the GA calipers on the fronts to have a nonbiased system, but I also intend on adjusting the proportioning to better balance the system and put more bias on the fronts, or however way the system should be set up.<P>On another note, I thought I recall reading somewhere of a setup for cars with GA calipers on the rears to regain the emergency brake. Not the lock system that can only be used for a parking brake, but an actual e-brake setup. I think it required adding a smaller manual caliper or something. Has anybody heard of such a setup?||||reg||5
Z||000018||Slammed Fiero||07-02-2001||09:32 PM||||My 11.25" vented disc conversion was finished today , complete with wilwood polymatrix pads , braided lines , Blazer Master cylinder. Brakes on par with a C2 911 and better than a c4 vette.<P>Was it cheap ..nope...cheaper than a pre manufacturered conversion though. Bigger than a Grand am setup , I had the rotors cryo treated as well.<P>As for an early comment on cross drilling , no it's not meant to save weight. It's meant to vent the gas that builds up between the pad and rotor , when the rotor gets hot. This gas is the source of brake fade. As for the weight reduction ,it's a bonus. But Cross drilled rotors are more of a look thing. They crack after a while. Get slotted rotors , they disapate heat better. and are not prone to the following. (Street driven mid 80's 911) <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/crackedrotors2.jpg"> <P>enjoy<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">       <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000019||West Coast Fiero||07-02-2001||09:47 PM||||<BR>Unfortunately our webpage has not ben updated in the past two weeks, however we offer brake upgrades ranging from Beretta brakes to Corvette brakes. We utilize bracketry that is manufactured here in house. We are able to do this because we are a machine shop first in foremost, the Fieros are a hobby for us. Therefore we are able to take our time to R&D essentially everything that we fabricate.<P> Granted this will only lead to more questions than answers that is why I ask that you either e-mail or call us for your questions as we will be able to answer them better <P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||07-03-2001||12:06 AM||||By "natural" balance, I mean the system has more front brake than rear. Even before any prop valve is added. That could mean a number of things depending on the system. In the case of Grand Am parts you have no natural balance. Only the Prop valve forced balance.<P>Keep in mind,,,, because of weight shift to the front, the front of a car is doing about 70% of the braking....<P>By comparison... A Front Only GA upgrade with the rear OE brakes and MC... is more in line with other systems and with Baer's recomended balance. (As I said above, a solid rear rotor isn't automatically bad... the majority of cars, in fiero weight class, with rear disk have solid rotors. Many cars is this weight class still use rear drum... Including some "sport," "gt," etc, versions)<P>((Yes, I am aware that there is a potential fluid volume problem with the OE MC and GA front calipers. There is a posible fix for this problem in my cave. No, I haven't had the time/money to test it. Being sick for a year s_cks.))<P>Since we didn't change rotor diameter or pad config in a significant way, we only have to deal with caliper pistons. If we change rotor working diameter, pad type, or pad surface area, then the math becomes much more complicated. Read Lawrence Mazza's brake article for some of those calculations...<P>Read the documents at <A HREF="http://www.Baer.com" TARGET=_blank>Baer's site</A>  especially their PDF(acrobat) downloads. Those will explain it better as far as pistons go. REad the last couple pages of the application guide carefully.<P>Slammed,,,<P>Drilling/slotting are used to save weight and for waste management. It depends what application the practice is used for. The drag people will shave every once they can... In general, neither is recomended for a street driven car. Baer and Wilwood will do it, but they consider it mainly an appearance item. Read the the doc's on those sites for their exact feelings on it.<P>It was originally done because the "binder" used to make certain pads generated gas that could literally lift the pads off the rotor. Certain high dust race pads today can have a similar problem.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||rc5star||07-03-2001||06:57 AM||||Eric (West Coast), do you keep these parts in stock ready to ship? How long does it usually take to recieve your parts? On average.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Will||07-03-2001||09:51 AM||||The proper brake balance of a car is very difficult to calculate, as it truly depends on both weight distribution and tire size, as well as expected g loads of a stop. Because of the weight distribution changes, the "correct" brake bias will be different for a 1.3 g stop than for a 0.8 g (low traction) stop.<P>The proper brake bias also depends on one other very important factor: the driver.<P>I have GA brake all around with performance friction carbomet pads. I like the setup a lot. It allows me to rotate the car with the brakes if I come into a tight low speed corner too fast.<BR>While the average Joe has no business coming into a tight low speed corner too fast, he might get himself in trouble trying the same braking maneuver. Joe's ideal brake balance would be more front-biased than mine.<P>I've got some math of my own to post, but no time right now.||||reg||1
Z||000023||West Coast Fiero||07-03-2001||11:04 AM||||the custom parts to adapt the brakes are kept on hand, however the GM parts, such as the calipers and pads are ordered per customer demand, and delivered to our customer, fulfilment of a certain brake upgrade may take 2 to 3 weeks||||reg||1
Z||000024||theogre||07-03-2001||11:25 PM||||Will,,,<P>If you would, please post that data into the "brake upgrade math" thread as soon as you can. I'd really like to see it and I'd like to keep all of it together. The direct link is in the first post of this thread.<P>All,<P>As I say in my big brake article.... application application applcation...<P>The driver is part of the application. Most people cannot safely handle the rear of the car coming around. It's not to be mean. It's a simple fact. It's something that is part instinct and part learned. (Also helps to live were there's lots of snow to play in...) You have to slide a car a whole bunch of times before you can get the feel of what the thing will do under given set of conditions. It's not something to try to learn on your own either.<P>This is allot of why most cars are setup to eliminate rear lockup as much as posible. Get's even more complicated with cars like Corvair and Fiero because of the rear mass. Worse yet in Fiero with added weight of a V8. (Corvair really was Unsafe at Any Speed.)<P>All of this is why I've done all the work on the big brake article and everything else. I want people to have brakes that are truely safe. In all but a few settings, I can't honestly call the 4 wheel Grand Am/Beretta setup such a system.<P>I started the whole thing because I want more brake on my car. I'm not willing to blindly follow directions without allot more to back it up than has been offered in most articles. Just as importantly,,, once you know what's going on, many of those articles have glairing mistakes in evalutaing the upgrade.<P>Example, paraphrased to keep short...<P>Article Author X<BR>I did the beretta upgrade on the front and installed the FS blazer MC to fix the fluid volume problem. Now I had too much front brake. So I installed the beretta(aka grand am) parts on the rear. Now I have better brake feel.<P>The whole reason Author X had too much front brake is that he seriously cut the rear brake power when he put on a bigger master cylinder. Had this author understood how step bore master cylinders work and how fiero's is installed he might have realized there are other ways to address MC volume.<P>The short rule goes,,,, increase wheel cyl bore and gain brake power, but increase MC bore and loose it. You lose a big chunk of brake power when the FS Blazer MC is used with the OE Fiero rear calipers. (In my cave article, This number is about the only on in red for a reason...)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-03-2001).]||||reg||1
